   "claims": [
    "All you need to do is use the board foot formula below: board feet = length (ft) × width (in) × thickness (in) / 12 Pay special attention to the units!",
    "For example, a piece of hardwood can be 8 feet long, 10 inches wide, and 1.25 inches thick.",
    "Assuming one board foot of lumber costs $4.15, then: cost = 41.67 × $4.15 = $172.92 💡 If you need to cover walls or flooring in plywood, check out our plywood calculator.",
    "You can also measure in feet; if so, don’t divide by 12 in step 5.",
    "A board foot is a volume of 1 ft × 1 ft × 1 in, so estimate the volume you need to fill before buying your spray foam insulation by finding the square footage of the space.",
    "A board foot of oak weighs anywhere between 3.08-4.67 lb (1.42-2.12 kg), but on average, one weighs 3.875 lb (1.77 kg).",
    "There are no board feet in 320 square feet, as board feet is a measure of volume and square feet is a measure of area.",
    "However, for an area that is 320 square feet with a depth of 1 inch, there are 320 board feet present."
   ],
